\subsection{Pair the Slopes, Not Just the Runs}

An instruction-selection experiment often asks a different question from
the add benchmark: did a replacement make the same unit of useful work
cheaper? For a steady-state test, use two repetition counts $n$ and $2n$
for each variant. Assume startup cost is approximately constant across
these counts for a given variant and that both runs stay in the same cache
and frequency regime. Report core cycles per repetition, where one
repetition computes the same checked result in either build. Do not
normalize by the number of instructions, which the replacement can change.

\begin{sudocode}[A two-size paired estimate]
\ForAll{measurement blocks $b$}
  \State $D\gets\emptyset$
  \ForAll{$(v,k)$ in balanced order over $\{A,B\}\times\{n,2n\}$}
    \State $D[v,k]\gets\Call{Measure}{v,k}$
    \If{result or run metadata is invalid}
      \State skip block $b$ \Comment{Never retain a partial pair}
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
  \State $s_A\gets(D[A,2n]-D[A,n])/n$
  \State $s_B\gets(D[B,2n]-D[B,n])/n$
  \State record $s_B-s_A$ \Comment{Negative favors replacement}
\EndFor
\State \Return median and spread of recorded differences
\end{sudocode}

For $n=1000$, suppose $A$ takes 8500 and 16,500 cycles, while $B$
takes 8400 and 15,600. The slopes are 8 and 7.2 cycles, so this block
contributes $-0.8$. The short run alone shows only a 100-cycle gain
because the variants have different fixed costs. If three valid blocks
yield $-0.8,-0.6,+0.2$, the median is $-0.6$; the positive result
is still worth reporting. A sample this small is not enough to establish
a stable gain. Rotate which variant and size go first across blocks and
log rejected blocks with their reasons; never reject a run merely because
it favors the baseline.

The invariant is that each difference uses four validated measurements
from one block on the same core, with the same event units. Doubling the
work may trigger a different regime even when the invariant holds.
Add a third count to check that adjacent slopes agree and repeat with a
different alignment. If the slopes disagree, report the size-dependent
result rather than fitting one cost for the selector.

